{"t":20.952358586,"dir":"in","msg":{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"figaro.aria","params":{"parts":[{"turn":1,"sealed":false,"live":{"from":0,"v":4,"nodes":[{"id":1,"set":{"args":{"content":"1. Il barbiere di Siviglia is an opera buffa in two acts by Gioachino Rossini.\n2. The Italian libretto is by Cesare Sterbini.\n3. It is based on Beaumarchais' 1775 comedy Le Barbier de Séville.\n4. The premiere took place at Rome's Teatro Argentina on 20 February 1816.\n5. Rossini composed it in a matter of weeks, reportedly under three.\n6. The opening night was a famous fiasco, disrupted by partisans of Giovanni Paisiello.\n7. Paisiello had set the same play in 1782, and his admirers resented the rival.\n8. To placate them, Rossini first titled his work Almaviva, ossia L'inutile precauzione.\n9. The second performance was a triumph, and the opera never looked back.\n10. The overture was recycled from earlier operas, including Aureliano in Palmira.\n11. Count Almaviva, a tenor, woos Rosina in disguise as the student Lindoro.\n12. Figaro, the baritone barber, is the city's indispensable factotum.\n13. Rosina, written for contralto, is often sung today by mezzo-sopranos.\n14. Doctor Bartolo, her guardian, schemes to marry her himself.\n15. Don Basilio, the music master, sings the aria \"La calunnia è un venticello.\"\n16. Figaro's entrance aria \"Largo al factotum\" is among the most famous in opera.\n17. Its patter refrain — \"Figaro qua, Figaro là\" — is instantly recognizable.\n18. Rosina answers with \"Una voce poco fa,\" a showcase for coloratura.\n19. Almaviva's disguises include a drunken soldier and a music teacher named Don Alonso.\n20. The Act I finale collapses into stupefied confusion: \"Freddo ed immobile.\"\n21. A thunderstorm interlude opens Act II's final scene.\n22. The lovers nearly flee, but the ladder is removed by Bartolo's own doing.\n23. Basilio is bribed with a ring to witness the marriage contract.\n24. Bartolo relents, consoled by keeping Rosina's dowry.\n25. The opera precedes Mozart's Le nozze di Figaro in story but follows it by thirty years.\n","path":"/var/tmp/rr/opera.md"},"summary":"content=1. Il barbiere di Siviglia is an opera buffa in two acts by Gioachino Rossini.\n2. The Italian libretto is by Cesare Sterbini.\n3. It is based on Beaumarchais' 1775 comedy Le Barbier de Séville.\n4. The premiere took place at Rome's Teatro Argentina on 20 February 1816.\n5. Rossini composed it in a matter of weeks, reportedly under three.\n6. The opening night was a famous fiasco, disrupted by partisans of Giovanni Paisiello.\n7. Paisiello had set the same play in 1782, and his admirers resented the rival.\n8. To placate them, Rossini first titled his work Almaviva, ossia L'inutile precauzione.\n9. The second performance was a triumph, and the opera never looked back.\n10. The overture was recycled from earlier operas, including Aureliano in Palmira.\n11. Count Almaviva, a tenor, woos Rosina in disguise as the student Lindoro.\n12. Figaro, the baritone barber, is the city's indispensable factotum.\n13. Rosina, written for contralto, is often sung today by mezzo-sopranos.\n14. Doctor Bartolo, her guardian, schemes to marry her himself.\n15. Don Basilio, the music master, sings the aria \"La calunnia è un venticello.\"\n16. Figaro's entrance aria \"Largo al factotum\" is among the most famous in opera.\n17. Its patter refrain — \"Figaro qua, Figaro là\" — is instantly recognizable.\n18. Rosina answers with \"Una voce poco fa,\" a showcase for coloratura.\n19. Almaviva's disguises include a drunken soldier and a music teacher named Don Alonso.\n20. The Act I finale collapses into stupefied confusion: \"Freddo ed immobile.\"\n21. A thunderstorm interlude opens Act II's final scene.\n22. The lovers nearly flee, but the ladder is removed by Bartolo's own doing.\n23. Basilio is bribed with a ring to witness the marriage contract.\n24. Bartolo relents, consoled by keeping Rosina's dowry.\n25. The opera precedes Mozart's Le nozze di Figaro in story but follows it by thirty years.\n path=/var/tmp/rr/opera.md"},"unset":["input"]}]},"from":0}],"more":{},"metrics":{"context_tokens":49,"context_limit":1000000,"context_exact":false,"tokens_in":0,"tokens_out":0,"cache_read_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"mantra":"Use the write tool exactly once to write /var/tmp/rr/opera.m…"}}}}
